# One construction lifecycle on the production Kingdom Quickstart road (ZAP-034 root ruling):
# real Quickstart boot, the same production quote/CanPay/commission Quickstart already drives
# during boot (Harness/KingdomQuickstartBuildTest.cs), then -- uniquely for this command --
# further AutoRunner lines that spend real engine turns to carry that exact paid job to a
# finished building, and a real save.
#
# WHY THE QUICKSTART ROAD, NOT founding-first-city (see lifecycle-founding-road-refusal.persona).
# On founding-first-city, `realize` places no camp kit and dedicates no stockpile: dedication is a
# founder-interactive act (Core/KingdomCharterPart.Vessels.cs) a sealed script never drives, so
# lifecycle-open there can only wait its bounded budget and honestly refuse.
# Harness/KingdomQuickstartLifecycleProvider.cs's own docstring gives the ORIGINAL reason that
# road was chosen instead of Quickstart: "A Quickstart run carries no scenario auto-runner ...
# Nothing in a Quickstart run can therefore spend engine turns." That was true until this
# persona's own wiring: Harness/KingdomQuickstartLifecycleRunnerPatch.cs now adds the runner to a
# Quickstart game, but ONLY for the separate quickstart-lifecycle command (never quickstart-boot,
# quickstart-save or quickstart-build), and Harness/KingdomScenarioAutoRunner.cs skips its usual
# re-strip and its usual line-0 verb for that same command -- the Quickstart camp's real founder
# stock must never be obliterated, and the command's own first line is boot syntax, not a verb.
# The root ruling overrides the original docstring reason because it targets the Quickstart
# STOCKPILE lifecycle by name: on this road the stockpile IS dedicated, by
# World/KingdomQuickstartBootstrap.Materials.cs:34, before the AutoRunner ever starts, so
# lifecycle-open's CanPay reads a real dedication and proceeds instead of waiting.
#
# NO lifecycle-build HERE. Quickstart's own boot-time build phase already ran the exact
# quote/CanPay/commission sequence for the lifecycle command (Harness/KingdomQuickstartBuildTest.cs,
# gated on KingdomQuickstartBootTest.LifecycleRequested) and persisted its job id into the SAME
# durable key KingdomQuickstartLifecycleSteps.Build publishes
# (KingdomQuickstartLifecycleSteps.JobKey) -- running lifecycle-build again would only refuse
# "this lifecycle already commissioned its one job". Tools/check-quickstart-lifecycle.py's own
# judge() already reads the QUICKSTART-BUILD-* rows this boot phase journals for the quote and
# paid-commission links (it prefers whichever named group has more matches present), so nothing
# needs to be re-driven.
#
# Nothing is fabricated: no stock is minted and no job is forced into a phase. lifecycle-grown
# refuses honestly when the turn budget expires before the building stands, exactly as on the
# founding road.
#
# NOT YET NATIVELY RUN: written and gate.sh-compiled only; this script has not been executed.
# The cold-load session and the next action after it are NOT in this script; see
# Tools/check-quickstart-lifecycle.py for why those two links still read BLOCKER without it.
DESCRIPTION=on the real Quickstart road the stockpile is already dedicated at boot, so lifecycle-open proceeds straight to a real engine-turn build and save
REQUEST=founding-first-city
SCRIPT=quickstart-lifecycle marsh yes;stagedigest;lifecycle-open;advance 2400;lifecycle-grown;lifecycle-save;stagedigest
VERBS=lifecycle-open,lifecycle-grown,lifecycle-save
EXPECT=QUICKSTART-BOOT-BEGIN:OK,QUICKSTART-BOOT-OBSERVED:OK,QUICKSTART-BOOT-COMPLETE:OK,QUICKSTART-BUILD-BEGIN:OK,QUICKSTART-BUILD-QUOTE:OK,QUICKSTART-BUILD-CANPAY:OK,QUICKSTART-BUILD-COMMISSION:OK,QUICKSTART-LIFECYCLE-PROFILE:OK,QUICKSTART-BUILD-COMPLETE:OK~commissioned=true,stagedigest:OK~founded=true,lifecycle-open:OK~step=startup,advance:OK,lifecycle-grown:OK~step=engine-turn-build,lifecycle-save:OK~step=save,stagedigest:OK~founded=true,COMPLETE
SET=growth,construction,native-regression
TIMEOUT=1200
